A||||0||William Federle||'84 Alternator Replacement||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||04-29-2000||08:01 AM||||I finally replaced my alternator. It worked but not well - the more things in the car I turned on, the lower the voltage went (and I checked with an external voltmeter). Also when starting on a cool morning the voltage for the first 10 seconds or so would be around 11 and the belt would squeal. I theorized that maybe when cold, the alternator wouldn't turn easily enough (though it seemed to turn easily enough when I got it out). I took the old one out by removing the adjusting lock bolt, then the oil filter,then the pivot bolt and nut, and then the two pivot bracket bolts. I should have left the locking bolt in loose until everything else was done. When I put the re-manufactured one in, I secured it to the adjusting bracket first, loosely, and then attached the pivot bracket and tightened, and then lined up the pivot bolt and tightened it. The socket for the two wire plug was in a different place - facing the coolant pipe that attaches to the manifold. Also the battery connection was in a slightly different place, but everything reached. Now I can have every single electrical device in the car on (fans at full speed) and the voltage never goes below 14V. Also, no squealing belt. The alternator is from Autozone and cost 75 dollars plus my old one.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 04-29-2000).]||||reg||1
